    <region left="0" top="10791" width="575" height="38" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>The results given at Table 2.2 are obtained by repeating the calculation for the three remaining <br />combinations of partial factors. </p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="10827" width="603" height="53" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>The maximal pressure on ground is achieved with the second combination, i.e. for the one in <br />which the wall self-weight and the self-weight of the ground above the footing are both multiplied by <br />1.35. </p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="0" top="10881" width="573" height="53" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>For the verification of the contact pressure, the possibility that the surcharge acts on the whole <br />embankment surface must be also considered. (Fig. 2.13); the values given at Table 2.3 are <br />obtained by repeating the calculation for this situation.</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
